{
  "title": "Plans and the future",
  "title_target": "Zer egingo duzu?",
  "goal": "Talk about plans with the future form -ko/-go, and say what you can, can't, have to, and want to do.",
  "bekal": [
    "form the future: *joango naiz*, *egingo dut*, *izango da*",
    "make plans with *asmoa dut*, *pentsatzen dut*, *agian*",
    "use *ahal*, *ezin*, *behar*, and *nahi* in a sentence"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Elkarrizketa — Udako planak",
      "intro": "Late June. Nerea and Budi sit on the cafeteria terrace, talking about the coming summer.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Zer egingo duzu udan?",
          "id": "What are you going to do this summer?",
          "literal": "what do-will you-have-it summer-in?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Indonesiara itzuliko naiz hilabete batez.",
          "id": "I'm going back to Indonesia for a month.",
          "literal": "Indonesia-to return-will I-am month one-for."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Ondo! Noiz joango zara?",
          "id": "Nice! When are you leaving?",
          "literal": "good! when go-will you-are?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Uztailaren hamabian. Abuztuan itzuliko naiz.",
          "id": "On July 12. I'll be back in August.",
          "literal": "July-of twelve-on. August-in return-will I-am."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Eta gero? Hemen geratuko zara?",
          "id": "And after that? Will you stay here?",
          "literal": "and later? here stay-will you-are?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Bai, beste urte batez. B1 maila lortu nahi dut.",
          "id": "Yes, for another year. I want to reach level B1.",
          "literal": "yes, other year one-for. B1 level reach want I-have-it."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Lortuko duzu, ziur nago.",
          "id": "You'll get there, I'm sure.",
          "literal": "reach-will you-have-it, sure I-am."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eskerrik asko! Eta zuk zer egingo duzu?",
          "id": "Thank you! And what are you going to do?",
          "literal": "thanks many! and you-k what do-will you-have-it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Uztailean lan egingo dut, eta abuztuan Frantziara joango naiz.",
          "id": "In July I'll work, and in August I'm going to France.",
          "literal": "July-in work do-will I-have-it, and August-in France-to go-will I-am."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Zeinen ondo! Bakarrik joango zara?",
          "id": "How great! Are you going alone?",
          "literal": "how good! alone go-will you-are?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Nerea:",
          "text": "Ez, lagunekin. Autoz joango gara.",
          "id": "No, with friends. We're going by car.",
          "literal": "no, friend-pl-with. car-by go-will we-are."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Primeran! Ondo pasa eta kontuz ibili.",
          "id": "Awesome! Have fun and be careful.",
          "literal": "awesome! well pass and careful walk."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**The future is made with -ko or -go; the auxiliary doesn't change.** *Joan* → *joan**go** naiz*, *egin* → *egin**go** dut*, *itzuli* → *itzuli**ko** naiz*. The auxiliary is exactly the same as in the present — only the verb gets something added. This is one of the easiest forms in Basque.",
        "**Hilabete batez — for a month.** The ending *-z* you already know (means, language, contents) also means \"for (a length of time)\": *hilabete batez* (for a month), *bi urtez* (for two years), *ordu betez* (for an hour).",
        "**Zeinen ondo! — how great.** *Zeinen* + adjective makes an exclamation: *zeinen polita!* (how pretty!), *zeinen zaila!* (so hard!). Another form that's just as common: *bai polita dela!*",
        "**Kontuz ibili — be careful.** Literally \"walk carefully\". Said to someone setting off on a trip, together with *ondo pasa* (have fun) and *bidaia on* (have a good trip). All three are among the most common goodbyes."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The future form",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Verb ending",
            "Add",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ends in a vowel or -i, -tu",
              "**-ko**",
              "itzuli → itzuli**ko**",
              "will return"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "hartu → hartu**ko**",
              "will take"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "ikasi → ikasi**ko**",
              "will learn"
            ],
            [
              "ends in -n",
              "**-go**",
              "joan → joan**go**",
              "will go"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "egin → egin**go**",
              "will do"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "izan → izan**go**",
              "will be"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "edan → edan**go**",
              "will drink"
            ],
            [
              "ends in -l, -r",
              "**-ko**",
              "hil → hil**ko**",
              "will die"
            ],
            [
              "",
              "",
              "ekarri → ekarri**ko**",
              "will bring"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "There's only one rule: if the verb ends in *-n*, add *-go*; otherwise add *-ko*. The auxiliary is exactly the same as in the present — *naiz*, *zara*, *da*, *dut*, *duzu*, *du*, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "tabel",
          "head": [
            "Present",
            "Future",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Etxera noa.",
              "Etxera **joango naiz**.",
              "I'll go home."
            ],
            [
              "Euskara ikasten dut.",
              "Euskara **ikasiko dut**.",
              "I'll learn Basque."
            ],
            [
              "Bihar klasea dago.",
              "Bihar klasea **egongo da**.",
              "There will be class tomorrow."
            ],
            [
              "Ez dut ezer egiten.",
              "**Ez dut** ezer **egingo**.",
              "I won't do anything."
            ],
            [
              "Zer egiten duzu?",
              "Zer **egingo duzu**?",
              "What will you do?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "When the present is enough",
          "items": [
            "For fixed plans with a stated time, the present is often used as is — just like English: *Bihar Donostiara noa* (\"Tomorrow I'm going to Donostia\").",
            "The *-ko/-go* form is used when the plan isn't settled yet, is far off, or you want to stress that it's in the future.",
            "For predictions and promises, the *-ko/-go* form is a must: *Lortuko duzu* (you'll make it), *Bihar euria egingo du* (it will rain tomorrow)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
